+### Two projects, two versions
+
+Identity is per project, and so is everything built on it. Two projects opened in
+one daemon hold their own registries, their own pins and their own runtime index,
+and both can run Epics at the same time on distributions with different digests —
+each admitted, dispatched and transited against its own project's registry, over
+the one worker pool the daemon has. Neither project's index records the other's
+distribution.
+
+That holds for extensions each project installs for itself. It does **not** yet
+hold for one extension installed globally and shared by both: the daemon serves
+the module it first imported for a path, so after a global update the second
+project does not get the new code — the loader keeps the identity of what is
+actually running and reports the drift in `project.diagnostics` rather than
+advertising bytes the process is not executing (see
+[The live-code hazard](#the-live-code-hazard)). Keeping the old version available
+for the first project while the second starts on the new one needs the
+distribution's bytes to be held, which this design records but does not yet store.
+
